# Cultural Landscapes Inventory: Little Round Top (NPS; text born digital and clean). Quotes cut by sentence number.
# Wall dates are the inventory's own: 'built pre-1863' or 'built 1863' and, where stated, the year the GBMA, park commission or NPS restored (restacked) the wall.
# A restored wall is NOT an 1863 profile (hard rule): its present height and shape are marked indirect.
@ NPS-CLI-LRT
%basis the inventory's citations (National Register documentation 2003 and 2014, Cultural Landscape Report 2012, Record of Treatment 2018, the 1868-69 Warren map)
29 | earthworks | Defensive stone walls built along the summit of Little Round Top and at Vincent Spur in 1863 | E-14,E-13,E-15 | 1863-07 | stated | The inventory's chronology records for 1863 that defensive stone walls were built along the summit of Little Round Top and at Vincent Spur | #s8
29 | fences | Existing stone fences become part of the battlefield in 1863 | P-14 | 1863-07 | stated | The existing stone fences (95th Pennsylvania, Munshower Field boundary, Weikert/Company B 20th Maine, John Weikert Lane, Nevins' Brigade walls) became integral parts of the battlefield in 1863 | #s7
75 | earthworks | Little Round Top stone walls: built 1863, boulders at hand, night of July 2 and morning of July 3 | E-14,E-13 | 1863-07-02 to 1863-07-03 | stated | Defensive stone walls lie across the summit and west slope; the boulder-strewn slopes gave Union infantry ample material to build stone breastworks during the night of July 2 and the morning of July 3 | #s3-4
75 | earthworks | Union forces extended and refined the walls until July 5 | E-14 | 1863-07-05 | stated | Throughout its occupation until July 5 Union forces extended and refined the Little Round Top walls | #s5
75 | earthworks | GBMA restacked the walls in 1882-1887: the extant walls are restored | E-14 | 1882-1887 restacking (modern profile, not 1863) | stated | Between 1882 and 1887 the GBMA restacked the stone walls, and most are extant today, providing evidence of the location of troops during the battle | #s6
75 | earthworks | Extant breastwork lines south and west of the summit: dry-laid diabase boulders and rubble | E-14 | 2020 condition (restored walls, not an 1863 profile) | stated | The nearly continuous lines of breastworks just south and west of the summit are dry-laid, irregular diabase granite boulders and rubble | #s7
75 | earthworks | Vincent Spur stone walls built 1863, restored 1890-1902 | E-14 | 1863-07 (built); 1890-1902 restored | stated | The Vincent Spur stone walls on the southeast slope were built by the Union army for defence in 1863 and restored in the 1890s | #s26
75 | fences | Nevins' Brigade stone wall built before 1863, restored 1907 | P-14 | pre-1863 (built); 1907 restored | stated | The north-south Nevins' Brigade stone wall marks the east side of the John Weikert Knoll parcel, was built before 1863, restored in 1907 and was probably used in battle positions | #s10
74 | fences | Field stone wall, Jacob Weikert farm / Company B 20th Maine: built before 1863 | P-14,bld-weikert-farm | pre-1863 (built) | stated | The north-south field stone wall just east of Vincent Spur, next to the 20th Maine Company B position marker, was built before 1863 | Field Stone Wall, Jacob Weikert Farm / Company B, 20th Maine Stone Wall (built pre-1863; Contributing) >>> 20th Maine Infantry Company B Position Marker.
74 | fences | Field stone wall, Company B 20th Maine: dry-laid, about 135 feet long and 2 feet high today | P-14 | 2020 measurement of the extant wall (modern; may be restored) | stated | The inventory gives the extant dry-laid wall as around 135 feet long and 2 feet high | #s16
74 | fences | John Weikert Lane stone wall built before 1863, restored 1907-1910 | P-14,bld-weikert-farm | pre-1863 (built); 1907-1910 restored | stated | The John Weikert Lane stone wall was built before 1863 and restored in 1907-1910; at the time of the battle it marked the west side of the John Weikert Knoll parcel and was probably used in battle positions | #s19-20
74 | fences | Boundary stone wall of the John Munshower Field, built before 1863 | P-14 | pre-1863 (built) | stated | The east-west wall along the north side of Wheatfield Road, built before 1863, enclosed the Munshower Field at the time of the battle and was probably used in battle positions | Boundary Stone Wall, John Munshower Field (built pre-1863; Contributing) This wall runs in an east-west direction along the north side of Wheatfield Road. At the time of the battle, this wall enclosed the Munshower Field and was likely incorporated into battle positions.
73 | fences | 95th Pennsylvania stone wall, east boundary of the John Weikert farm, built before 1863 | P-14 | pre-1863 (built) | stated | The 95th Pennsylvania stone wall runs north-south perpendicular to Wheatfield Road and forms the east boundary of the John Weikert/Althoff farm; it was built before 1863 | 95th Pennsylvania Stone Wall (portion) (built pre-1863; Contributing) This stone wall runs in a north-south direction, perpendicular to Wheatfield Road, and forms the eastern boundary of John Weikert/Althoff Farm.
73 | fences | Stone walls restored or replaced; new wall sections at the Sykes Avenue parking lot | P-14 | 2020 condition (restored or replaced; not 1863) | stated | The inventory says stone walls and fences that existed at the time of the battle at Little Round Top have been restored or replaced, including defensive walls, and that new wall sections now stand at the Sykes Avenue lot | #s20 | #s6
66 | roads | Jacob Weikert Woods Access Lane present by 1863 | bld-weikert-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The lane on the east slope of Little Round Top was present during the battle and likely used by the Union army, the Company B stone wall being nearby | #s12
53 | woodlots | The western side of Little Round Top was open at the time of the battle | - | 1863-07 | stated | At the time of the battle the western side of Little Round Top was open, giving observation and fields of fire from the summit to the west, northwest and southwest | #s5
58 | woodlots | Emanuel Weikert Woods wooded during the battle | - | 1863-07 | stated | The portion of the Emanuel Weikert Woods along Wright Avenue was wooded during the battle and the site of heavy fighting | #s4-5
58 | woodlots | Today's Little Round Top is managed to look as in July 1863 | - | 2020 management (modern) | stated | The inventory says Little Round Top is maintained to appear much as in July 1863, keeping the field on the western slope and letting the dry oak-mixed hardwood forest of the eastern slope mature | #s2
59 | fields | Old field on the west slope: today's species show a slightly more mature old field than in 1863 | - | 1863-07 (old field); today more mature | stated | The present species of the west-slope field (smooth sumac, eastern red cedar mixed with grasses) indicate a slightly more mature old field than what existed in 1863 | #s17
52 | woodlots | Natural woodland and thickets in bottomlands; Plum Run vegetation at the time of the battle | - | 1863-07 | stated | While managed fields, pastures, orchards and woodlots dominated the 1863 landscape, pockets of naturally occurring woodland and thickets grew in the bottomlands and along streams, at Little Round Top along Plum Creek | #s19-20
56 | fields | Battle-era agricultural landscape: crops, meadows, pastures | - | 1863-07 | stated | Many local fields were used for crops, meadows or pastures, which gave varying patterns of texture, colour, density and height, and crops were planted where soils were rich and well drained | #s6-7
56 | fields | Most fields under 15 acres; more than 3,500 acres of open fields within the present park | - | 1863-07 | stated | Most fields were fairly small, under 15 acres, but in the battle era there were more than 3,500 acres of open fields within the present park boundaries | #s8
72 | fences | No farmstead at Little Round Top; field fences of wood and stone or stone only | - | 1863-07 | stated | In the Little Round Top area no farm building clusters stood during the battle, but farmers' fences enclosed and divided the fields; on the rough rocky ground they were wood and stone or exclusively stone, with chestnut and locust the preferred woods | #s10-12
81 | general | The battle was fought in a mosaic of open fields, semi-open woodlots and dense woodlands | - | 1863-07 | stated | The 1863 battle was largely fought within a mosaic of open fields, semi-open woodlots and dense woodlands interspersed with roads and lanes, wood and stone fences, and farm buildings | #s3
81 | woodlots | Tree growth after the war obscured views by 1890 | - | after 1863 | stated | By 1890 certain points and views were being obscured by tree growth that had not existed at the time of the battle | #s10
63 | fences | Park rehabilitation since 1999: 17 miles of historic fences rebuilt, orchards, woodlots, thickets replanted | - | 1999-2020 rehabilitation (modern) | stated | Since 1999 the park has rebuilt over 17 miles of historic fences, re-established 325 acres of fields, 18 acres of riparian buffer and replanted 112 acres of orchards, 49 acres of woodlots and 28 acres of thickets present at the time of the battle | #s18
